(2219503, 299441, 9906, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>This is a very hard shot for me to critique because, through no fault of the photographer, it doesn''t hold much interest for me...  I do like the idea for the challenge though.<br/> <br/>What I like about it:  The overall concept for the challenge is interesting.  It''s kinda wierd how you can see something out and about and then a couple of days later there''s a challenge that fits it...  I like the shadow in the frame, but it looks a little off to me.  Kind of a blueish cast to it that keeps drawing my attention.<br/> <br/>What I don''t like:  I don''t like how the bottom half of the shot (where the subject matter is) is so dark compared to the top half of the shot (the negative space so to speak).  A little fill light would help that out.  Perhaps as simple as some reflected light.  The window in the top left corner is very distracting as well as the line just under it at the side of the frame and above the gray wall.  The spot on the wall at about 1:00 at the very top draws my eye also.  The subjects themselves look like they have some awesome detail work on them.  However, because they are so small it''s very hard to see it.  Also because the primary light is coming at them so directly, it makes them look a little flat.  This light also makes rather harsh shadows behind their heads.<br/> <br/>I think that this is a very interesting scene and would love to see you revisit it at different times and different angles and see what else you can come up with.  I think this would be a very cool pic if you could catch it with a bit of snow on it...<br/> <br/>Keep shooting!<br/>Yours<br/>TC</td>', 'From the Critique Club: This is a very hard shot for me to critique because, through no fault of the photographer, it doesn''t hold much interest for me...  I do like the idea for the challenge though. What I like about it:  The overall concept for the challenge is interesting.  It''s kinda wierd how you can see something out and about and then a couple of days later there''s a challenge that fits it...  I like the shadow in the frame, but it looks a little off to me.  Kind of a blueish cast to it that keeps drawing my attention. What I don''t like:  I don''t like how the bottom half of the shot (where the subject matter is) is so dark compared to the top half of the shot (the negative space so to speak).  A little fill light would help that out.  Perhaps as simple as some reflected light.  The window in the top left corner is very distracting as well as the line just under it at the side of the frame and above the gray wall.  The spot on the wall at about 1:00 at the very top draws my eye also.  The subjects themselves look like they have some awesome detail work on them.  However, because they are so small it''s very hard to see it.  Also because the primary light is coming at them so directly, it makes them look a little flat.  This light also makes rather harsh shadows behind their heads. I think that this is a very interesting scene and would love to see you revisit it at different times and different angles and see what else you can come up with.  I think this would be a very cool pic if you could catch it with a bit of snow on it... Keep shooting!YoursTC', '2006-03-11 16:19:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(2219903, 300866, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Wow 11th place, great job and congrats!  What is even better is this is a great scoring shot and it is not even in your top four.   You have been rocking.  Ok... on the challenge itself you have captured the essence of an odd couple.  <br/> <br/>The image is a great idea.  Two very different people who are obviously connected.  The lighting you used is very nice.  The soft feel is very appealing to be, it gives almost a mystical feel especially to her feet.  The composition is very and feels complete.  The only thing I could see people voting the phot higher for is a little less blur, but then again that is probably way some people voted the way they did.  There is not much I can say but great job and keep up the excellent work.</td>', '*Critique Club* Wow 11th place, great job and congrats!  What is even better is this is a great scoring shot and it is not even in your top four.   You have been rocking.  Ok... on the challenge itself you have captured the essence of an odd couple.   The image is a great idea.  Two very different people who are obviously connected.  The lighting you used is very nice.  The soft feel is very appealing to be, it gives almost a mystical feel especially to her feet.  The composition is very and feels complete.  The only thing I could see people voting the phot higher for is a little less blur, but then again that is probably way some people voted the way they did.  There is not much I can say but great job and keep up the excellent work.', '2006-03-11 20:02:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(2220878, 300271, 49873, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Neuferland:</b></div><hr/><i> Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Hello!  I see this is your third entry to the challenges!  Welcome!<br/> <br/>This is a perfect example of the Odd Couple, your idea was perfect.  So why didn''t you win a ribbon?  Well, as most of the comments said, the lighting is a bit off.  I thought the same thing when I first saw this.  If there was a way you could have put a white backdrop over the record player and really bumped up the lights on this and sharpened the focus just a touch this could have blown the others out of the water.  If you ever try to redo this shot I would love to see the results.<br/> <br/>Hope my comments help and good luck in future challenges!<br/> <br/>Deannda </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks a lot Deannda, your comment was very very helpful. I was trying to figure out what was wrong with the lightning and how to correct it. You explained it so well. I will try another shot with your suggestion. There are lots to learn for a newbie like me :)</td>', 'Originally posted by Neuferland: Greetings from the Critique Club! Hello!  I see this is your third entry to the challenges!  Welcome! This is a perfect example of the Odd Couple, your idea was perfect.  So why didn''t you win a ribbon?  Well, as most of the comments said, the lighting is a bit off.  I thought the same thing when I first saw this.  If there was a way you could have put a white backdrop over the record player and really bumped up the lights on this and sharpened the focus just a touch this could have blown the others out of the water.  If you ever try to redo this shot I would love to see the results. Hope my comments help and good luck in future challenges! Deannda   Thanks a lot Deannda, your comment was very very helpful. I was trying to figure out what was wrong with the lightning and how to correct it. You explained it so well. I will try another shot with your suggestion. There are lots to learn for a newbie like me :)', '2006-03-12 05:15:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(2228496, 300486, 35334, '<td valign="top">Sherryl,<br/> <br/>You have an excellent eye for composition. The shapes in the clouds echo the shapes in the water and both draw the eye to the taller man, thus emphasizing the contrast between their sizes. The result is a very powerful image that I like very much.<br/> <br/>I left a comment on your Square Crop image in which I said that the use of overly bright fill flash weakened the image. This image was also taken with strong fill flash--and I also think that it would be better without it. The natural light in the scene is shining towards the front of the two men so their backs should be in shadow. But their backs are brightly lit and there are even shadows going towards the apparent light source. To me, this gives the image an unnatural quality. If you had turned off the flash, maybe it would even have gotten a ribbon.<br/> <br/>--Dan</td>', 'Sherryl, You have an excellent eye for composition. The shapes in the clouds echo the shapes in the water and both draw the eye to the taller man, thus emphasizing the contrast between their sizes. The result is a very powerful image that I like very much. I left a comment on your Square Crop image in which I said that the use of overly bright fill flash weakened the image. This image was also taken with strong fill flash--and I also think that it would be better without it. The natural light in the scene is shining towards the front of the two men so their backs should be in shadow. But their backs are brightly lit and there are even shadows going towards the apparent light source. To me, this gives the image an unnatural quality. If you had turned off the flash, maybe it would even have gotten a ribbon. --Dan', '2006-03-14 23:47:01.000000', NULL, 0),
